+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+# Self-host Fizzy with Kamal
+
+This configuration runs Fizzy on one Linux server: the Rails web process, Solid Queue background workers, all four SQLite databases, and locally stored uploads. Kamal Proxy terminates HTTPS and obtains a Let's Encrypt certificate. No MySQL, Redis, object storage, or separate jobs server is required.
+
+## Requirements
+
+- A Linux server with Docker and SSH access from the machine you deploy from. Kamal connects as `root` by default; for a non-root deploy user, set `ssh.user` in `config/deploy.yml` and grant that user Docker access. The server must be reachable by the hostname used below.
+- A DNS `A` (and, if applicable, `AAAA`) record for your Fizzy hostname pointing to the server. Allow inbound TCP ports 22, 80, and 443; ports 80 and 443 are needed by Kamal Proxy and Let's Encrypt.
+- An SMTP account that can send mail from an address on your domain. Fizzy sends sign-in links by email.
+- Docker available on the machine running Kamal so it can build the image.
+- Ruby and Bundler installed locally, with this repository's gems installed (`bundle install`).
+
+## Configure and deploy
+
+1. Edit `config/deploy.yml`:
+   - Replace `fizzy.example.com` with the hostname users will visit. It is both the SSH address and the public app host; if you SSH to a different address, change only the entry under `servers.web`.
+   - Set `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_DOMAIN`, and `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION` to the values supplied by your mail provider. Port 587 with STARTTLS is the default setup. For implicit TLS (usually port 465), set `SMTP_TLS` to `"true"`; otherwise use `SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO` to control STARTTLS.
+   - Set `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` to a sender address verified by your provider.
+   - Change `builder.arch` to `arm64` if your server is ARM-based.
+2. Create your local secrets file and edit it with your SMTP credentials:
+
+   ```sh
+   cp .kamal/secrets.example .kamal/secrets
+   bin/rails secret
+   bin/rails runner 'key = WebPush.generate_key; puts "VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{key.private_key}"; puts "VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{key.public_key}"'
+   ```
+
+   Put the output of `bin/rails secret` in `SECRET_KEY_BASE`, the SMTP username and password in `SMTP_USERNAME` and `SMTP_PASSWORD`, and the generated VAPID values in `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` and `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY`. Keep `.kamal/secrets` private; it is git-ignored. Do not regenerate `SECRET_KEY_BASE` or the VAPID keys for later deployments. Quote dotenv values if they contain whitespace or `#` characters.
+3. Make sure the DNS record is live and the server is accessible over SSH, then run:
+
+   ```sh
+   bin/kamal setup
+   ```
+
+   Kamal builds Fizzy, creates a local image registry, starts the proxy, and deploys the app. Once the proxy has obtained its certificate, open `https://your-hostname`.
+
+For subsequent releases, run `bin/kamal deploy`.
+
+## Persistence and background jobs
+
+Kamal mounts the named Docker volume `fizzy_storage` at `/rails/storage`. Fizzy keeps its primary, cache, Action Cable, and Solid Queue SQLite databases under that directory, as well as Active Storage files, so replacing the app container on a deploy does not replace the data. Do not remove this volume when cleaning up Docker resources. It is still a single-server deployment: back up the volume separately to protect against server or disk loss.
+
+Only the `web` role is configured. `config/puma.rb` starts Solid Queue inside Puma by default, so scheduled and queued background jobs run on the same server. The deployment explicitly keeps Solid Queue enabled in Puma and sets web and job concurrency to one process to suit a small single-box installation; adjust those values if the server has more capacity. Do not set `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA=false` or add a separate jobs role unless you intentionally change this layout.
